WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.290
First build the rock sequence.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:06.710
Sediment accumulates, depositional environments change, and the layers become rock.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:11.690
When uplift or retreating seas exposes the sequence, erosion begins removing

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:15.950
material. A river can cut downward while tributaries and slope failure

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:19.430
widen the canyon. The old rock and the young canyon record

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.130
different events.

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:24.510
An erosion surface must postdate the rocks it truncates.

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:28.070
If a lava flow later covers that surface, the surface must

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:29.770
already exist before the eruption.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:33.410
Dating the rock below and the lava above places limits on

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:37.390
the surface age. Neither number automatically dates the moment erosion began

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.110
or ended.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.270
A stream terrace preserves a former stream level above the modern

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.150
channel. Incision left that older surface behind.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:48.370
Undisturbed surfaces also develop soil over time.

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:52.170
Thick soil on an upland and little soil on recent stream

00:00:52.170 --> 00:00:55.450
sediment support a relative comparison in the supplied example.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.990
Climate and disturbance must still be considered.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:02.970
Desert varnish accumulates iron and manganese oxides on exposed rock.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.850
Desert pavement develops as fine material leaves or moves downward and

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:08.890
larger clasts concentrate near the surface.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:13.650
Dark varnish and a well-developed pavement can indicate long exposure, but

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:17.650
abrasion or renewed deposition can interrupt or reset that record.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:21.990
Cosmic rays can produce measurable isotopes in exposed stones.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.250
Laboratory analysis can constrain exposure duration.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:30.330
Combine that evidence with buried surfaces, terraces, soils and varnish rather

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:33.590
than substituting bedrock age for landscape age.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:37.210
Ask what started the clock, what could disturb it, and whether

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.130
the measured event is formation, exposure or burial.
